  "problem": "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a=a\\cdot a$\n- $b\\cdot a<0$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\".",
